'''3.''' To agitate; disturb; overturn or throw into commotion; hence, to dispute; contest; cause to be discussed: in this sense chiefly used in the past participle: as, a ''vexed'' (much discussed but unsettled) question.

{{smaller block|<poem>He was met even now
As mad as the ''vex’d'' sea. ''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 4. 2.</poem>}}

{{smaller block|How are endless fields ''vexed'' with ploughshares!
{{right|''Channing'', Perfect Life, p. 157.}}}}

{{smaller block|Not ''vexing'' a question (settled forever without our votes).
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, xli.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>No thought of storm the morning ''vexes'' yet.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 291.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' ''Annoy, Plague,'' etc. (see ''tease''), provoke, gall, chafe.—'''3.''' To disquiet.}}

'''II.†''' ''intrans.'' To fret; be teased or irritated; feel annoyed, angry, or distressed.

{{smaller block|<poem>I do command thee be my slave forever,
And ''vex'' while I laugh at thee.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', False One, iv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Prithee, sweet Mistress Dorothy, ''vex'' not; how much is it [a debt]? ''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, ii. 1.}}

'''vex''' (veks), ''n.'' [< ''vex'', ''v.''] A trouble; a vexation. [Scotch.]

{{smaller block|My mother gar’d me learn the Single Carritch, whilk was a great ''vex''. ''Scott'', Old Mortality, xxxvii.}}

{{smaller block|A sair ''vex'' to mony a . . . body.
{{right|''Geo. MacDonald'', Warlock o’ Glenwarlock, xliii.}}}}

