'''vexillary''' (vek′si-lạ̄-ri), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''vexillarius'', a standard-bearer: see ''vexillar''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Same as ''vexillar'', 1.—'''2.''' In ''bot.'', of or pertaining to the vexillum or standard.—'''Vexillary estivation''', {{smaller|a mode of estivation in which the exterior petal, as in the case of the vexillum, is largest, and incloses and folds over the other petals.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' One who carries a vexillum; a standard-bearer.
{{smaller block|<poem>Letters like to those the ''vexillary''
Hath left crag-carven o’er the streaming Gelt.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

'''vexillate''' (vek′si-lāt), ''a.'' [< ''vexill(um)'' + ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>.] Having vexilla or pogonia; webbed or pogoniate, as a feather.

'''vexillation''' (vek-si-lā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''vexillatio(n-)'', a body of soldiers under one standard, a battalion, < ''vexillum'', a standard: see ''vexillum''.] A company of troops under one vexillum or ensign.

'''vexillator''' (vek′si-lā-tọr), ''n.'' [ML., < L. ''vexillum'', a standard: see ''vexillum''.] A standard-bearer. See the quotations.
{{smaller block|In manner of representation there was no essential difference between the performance of a morality and that of a miracle; the pageants used for one were used for the other; ''vexillators'' proclaimed the intended performance, and the performers went from place to place, in both cases. {{right|''A. W. Ward'', Eng. Dram. Lit., I. 58.}}}}
{{smaller block|The prologue to this curious drama [“Corpus Christi”] is delivered by three persons, who speak alternately, and are called ''vexillators''. {{right|''Strutt'', Sports and Pastimes, p. 229.}}}}
